A static variable in a function is local to the function but it remains in memory for the duration of program execution. Its lifetime is until the termination of the program.

The value stored in it is retained for the next function call and it preserves the value of variable.

It will be zero if it is not explicitly initialized.

static variables are initialized to zero if no value is specified at the time of declaration.

Register Variables:

Variables which are declared with register keyword are called as register variables.

It stores the variable in one of the CPU registers for faster access.

Register variables are local to the block in which they are declared.

The variable type must be either integer or character. Pointers and float types are not allowed.

If there is a sufficient number of available registers, the compiler will store the variable in one of them.

If there is no sufficient number of available registers, the compiler will store the variable in the memory.

The address-of operator (&) cannot be used on a register variable.

Register variables are useful for frequently accessed variables or small loops.

They are faster than the other variables because they store the variable in the CPU register.

They are declared using the register keyword.

