Implementing multiple-choice dialogue is a great way to add interactivity to a text-based game. The core concept is simple: store the conversation data, display the current options, and listen for player input to update the state of the conversation.

Here are three approaches, ranging from a beginner-friendly manual implementation to a scalable object-oriented design.

---

### Approach 1: The "State Machine" (Beginner Friendly)

In this approach, we store dialogue lines in an array or list and use a counter to track which line is currently showing. When the player chooses an option, we calculate the index of the next line.

**Pros:** Very easy to understand logic.
**Cons:** Hard to manage complex conversations (loops, branching paths become messy).

#### Code Example

```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class SimpleDialogue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Define the lines of dialogue
        String[] dialogue = {
            "You enter a dark room.",
            "A shadowy figure says: 'Who goes there?'",
            "You can approach or run away.",
            "Approach: 'I am a traveler.'",
            "Run: 'Get back!' (You run out the door.)",
            "The figure chuckles: 'Very well.'"
        };

        int currentNode = 0;
        boolean running = true;

        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.println("GAME START");
        System.out.println(dialogue[0]);

        while (running) {
            // Print the current line
            System.out.println("\n[Line " + currentNode + "]: " + dialogue[currentNode]);

            if (currentNode == 1) {
                // This line offers a choice
                System.out.println("1. Approach");
                System.out.println("2. Run Away");
                System.out.print("Choice: ");

                String choice = scanner.nextLine();
                if (choice.equals("1")) {
                    currentNode = 3; // Jump to the "Approach" line
                } else if (choice.equals("2")) {
                    currentNode = 4; // Jump to the "Run" line
                } else {
                    currentNode = 1; // Invalid input, stay on this node
                    continue;
                }
            } 
            else if (currentNode == 2 || currentNode == 4) {
                // End nodes (No next line logic needed here for simplicity)
                running = false;
            }

            // If the game isn't over, move to the next sequential line
            if (running) {
                currentNode++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Game Over.");
    }
}
```

---

### Approach 2: Object-Oriented (Recommended)

For a real game,
